{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Matuschek1998-chirped-not-matched",
  "Source": "N. Matuschek, F. X. Kärtner, and U. Keller, \"Theory of double-chirped mirrors,\" IEEE J. Sel. Top. Quantum Electron. 4, 197-208 (1998). doi:10.1109/2944.686723 - Sec. IV-C text p. 203 and Fig. 6 / Fig. 7(a) caption (dashed and dotted curves); also Fig. 2 dotted curves",
  "Notes": "Simple linearly chirped (Bragg wavenumber) dielectric mirror, quarter-wave index steps, impedance not matched\nSpecification: Broad high reflectivity with negative GDD (illustrative example scalable to any range; chosen for sub-10-fs Ti:sapphire)\nReported: Highly reflective from k/k0 = 0.59-1.03 (580-1020 nm); strong group-delay oscillations with amplitude up to +/-25 fs; GD singularity of the WKB solution near k/k0 = 0.85 (Fig. 7(a)).\nNotes: Construction (Sec. IV-C, Figs. 3, 6, 7): 25 symmetric index steps, each = 1/2 d_l | d_h | 1/2 d_l (Fig. 3), n_h = 2.5, n_l = 1.5, no dispersion. Bragg wavelength of step |m| (|m| = 1 at the front, where light enters): k_B = k0 - (|m|-1)(k0 - kmin)/19 for |m| = 1..20 with k0 = 2pi/600 nm, kmin = 2pi/900 nm; |m| = 21..25 kept at kmin. Bragg wavelength = 2 (n_h d_h + n_l d_l). The paper computes reflectivity with respect to the first low-index (SiO2-like) layer and explicitly ignores the jump to air, so the incident medium is taken as n = 1.5 (the front half-low layer merges into it); the back half-low layer merges into the n = 1.5 (SiO2) substrate. Adjacent half low layers of steps m and m+1 are merged, giving 49 layers (25 H, 24 L). Thicknesses computed by me from the stated formulas (rounded to 0.001 nm); layer 1 = front (incident side), last H layer is next to the substrate. Here d_h = lambda_B/(4 n_h), d_l = lambda_B/(4 n_l) for every step.\nLayers from Sec. IV-C text p. 203 and Fig. 6 / Fig. 7(a) caption (dashed and dotted curves); also Fig. 2 dotted curves; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R >= 98.6 % from 580 to 1020 nm; paper highly reflective 580-1020 nm.",
  "Wavelength": [
    600
  ],
